  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 252 PM EDT Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Isolated to scattered showers with a couple of \nthunderstorms remain on track for this evening into the overnight \nhours. For late this afternoon and evening, it appears our setup is \nreminiscent of yesterday with pulse storm potential. However, subtle \ndifferences will limit the thunderstorm threat. Instability over the \nAdirondack region is less robust, and so the ability for stronger \nconvection will be limited by these initial weaker updrafts. \nHowever, increasing cyclonic flow as an upper level wave over the \neastern Great Lakes gets slowly pushed towards our region will help \ngenerate more showers with time, peaking during the early morning \nhours and across northern portions of Vermont. With such weak \nforcing including lack of a well defined front or low pressure \nsystem, precipitation amounts will continue to be quite \nspatially variable. Activity will generally shift to the \nnortheast with time, and pockets of heavier rainfall (~0.25 - \n0.5\" with localized 1\" amounts) will tend occur through tomorrow\nmorning. Some locations, probably in southern portions of \nVermont, again should see little or no rain. \n\nFor Saturday, there are again some mixed signals in terms of the \nconvective evolution. Each of the last couple of cycles, machine \nlearning guidance has been somewhat bullish on potential for some \nstronger thunderstorms. Consensus shows higher probabilities both \nsouth and east of our region; the latest Day 2 Storm Prediction \nCenter severe risk does highlight the mid-Atlantic region again \nfollowing today's stronger storms in that area as well. That being \nsaid, generally thunderstorm probabilities are roughly as high in \nour region as towards the south, with regionally greatest chances in \nnortheastern Vermont and lowest chances in the southern St. Lawrence \nValley and southern Vermont. Representative forecast soundings in \nSt. Johnsbury during the early evening show MLCAPE of around 1500 \nJ/kg (moderately high CAPE) and effective shear of 10-15 knots (low \nwind shear), so the environment looks marginally supportive of pulse \nthunderstorms with mainly typical thunderstorms and a rare stronger \nwind gust possible. \n\nFollowing yesterday's anomalous moisture, precipitable water will be \na bit lower tonight when it surges with the wave coming through \n(about 1.7\" to 1.9\"), and fall further tomorrow (about 1.5\" to \n1.7\"). So while a marginal (level 1 out of 5) risk of flash flooding \ncontinues associated with heavy rainfall rates in thunderstorms, \nthink the footprints of 2\" + that we saw yesterday are less likely. \nWith meager instability available ahead of a final round \nof scattered showers moving through overnight Saturday into the \nday on Sunday, risk of impactful weather diminishes further as the \nweekend wears on. As the associated weak surface front passes \nto our east, sharply drier, refreshing air will return during the \nsecond part of the day.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Tranquil, seasonably warm conditions from late Sunday \ninto Monday will prevail as a narrow axis of deep layer ridging\nzips into and then out of our region to be replaced by a \nprefrontal trough Monday evening. Most of the impacts from this \ntrough and the following cold front will follow Monday night, \nbut overall the threat of showers and thunderstorms will ramp up\ntowards evening, particularly in northernmost portions of New \nYork where both rain and thunder chances are highest through the\nafternoon hours. Stronger storms currently look unlikely with \nonly modest instability and shear, but we'll have to monitor any\ntrends in the data if they deviate from the current thinking.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: A cold front is expected to cross the region Monday \nnight, bringing an increased chance (45-85%) of precipitation to \nnorthern New York and Vermont, including some overnight embedded \nthunder. Temperatures will be on the milder side throughout the \nnight, remaining at or above the mid 50s to mid 60s. Tuesday could \nalso be a showery day with embedded thunder (precip chances 15-55%) \nunder modest cold air advection and seasonable temperatures in the \nmid 70s to mid 80s. Mid week will feature a period of 15-55% chance \nof precipitation and seasonable temperatures (highs mid 70s to lower \n80s, lows 50s and lower 60s). Then, longwave troughing returns late \nweek, bringing a resurgence of higher shower and thunderstorm \nchances on Thursday afternoon plus highs falling into the 70s for \nmost both Thursday and Friday.",
